{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01MdKZZ23Xgrvu3DNxRKYz48", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "[id 36] @mia_on_two_wheels: My saddle height was wrong for a year. Fixed it and my knees thank me every single ride. (likes: 7)\n[id 65] @fixie_fran: Rewrapped my bars in zebra-print tape today. Tip: start at the bar end, overlap by a third, and finish with electrical tape under the plug. (likes: 48)\n[id 37] @mia_on_two_wheels: Lights, bell, fenders, lock. A commuter bike is a tool and good tools get looked after. (likes: 11)\n[id 66] @fixie_fran: My commuter is nicknamed the Zebra because of the mismatched black and white fenders. Re-greased the headset this weekend, steering feels brand new. (likes: 38)\n[id 14] @chainlube_carla: Squeaky chain after rain? Dry it with a rag, then a light coat of lube. Do not just add more lube on top of the grime. (likes: 11)\n[id 53] @brakebleed_bea: Rotor rubbing? Loosen the caliper bolts, squeeze the lever, tighten while holding it. Centered in seconds. (likes: 5)\n[id 6] @spokewrench_sam: Rim tape matters. A cheap strip that shifts will give you mystery flats from the spoke holes no matter how careful you are. (likes: 11)\n[id 1] @spokewrench_sam: Truing a wheel takes patience: quarter turns, both sides, check the dish every few spokes. Rushing it is how you end up with a potato chip. (likes: 7)\n[id 59] @hubservice_hal: Freehub clicking turned into grinding. Pulled it apart, cleaned the pawls, light oil, back to clicking. (likes: 9)\n[id 71] @fixie_fran: Painted my old steel frame in black and white stripes, zebras would approve. Prep: strip, sand to 240, degrease, thin coats. Zebra paint job done. (likes: 62)\n[id 33] @mia_on_two_wheels: First time adjusting my own brakes and it actually worked. Barrel adjuster, small turns, squeeze to check, repeat. (likes: 4)\n[id 22] @trueing_stand_tom: Tubeless rims are stiffer to true but they hold their shape much longer once you get them right. (likes: 6)"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a064f315-9694-4c3e-b095-0c64f8f09804", "uuid": "b82ea28e-291f-45c4-99f2-8eee9273374a", "timestamp": "2026-10-30T21:53:33.592Z", "tool_use_result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "[id 36] @mia_on_two_wheels: My saddle height was wrong for a year. Fixed it and my knees thank me every single ride. (likes: 7)\n[id 65] @fixie_fran: Rewrapped my bars in zebra-print tape today. Tip: start at the bar end, overlap by a third, and finish with electrical tape under the plug. (likes: 48)\n[id 37] @mia_on_two_wheels: Lights, bell, fenders, lock. A commuter bike is a tool and good tools get looked after. (likes: 11)\n[id 66] @fixie_fran: My commuter is nicknamed the Zebra because of the mismatched black and white fenders. Re-greased the headset this weekend, steering feels brand new. (likes: 38)\n[id 14] @chainlube_carla: Squeaky chain after rain? Dry it with a rag, then a light coat of lube. Do not just add more lube on top of the grime. (likes: 11)\n[id 53] @brakebleed_bea: Rotor rubbing? Loosen the caliper bolts, squeeze the lever, tighten while holding it. Centered in seconds. (likes: 5)\n[id 6] @spokewrench_sam: Rim tape matters. A cheap strip that shifts will give you mystery flats from the spoke holes no matter how careful you are. (likes: 11)\n[id 1] @spokewrench_sam: Truing a wheel takes patience: quarter turns, both sides, check the dish every few spokes. Rushing it is how you end up with a potato chip. (likes: 7)\n[id 59] @hubservice_hal: Freehub clicking turned into grinding. Pulled it apart, cleaned the pawls, light oil, back to clicking. (likes: 9)\n[id 71] @fixie_fran: Painted my old steel frame in black and white stripes, zebras would approve. Prep: strip, sand to 240, degrease, thin coats. Zebra paint job done. (likes: 62)\n[id 33] @mia_on_two_wheels: First time adjusting my own brakes and it actually worked. Barrel adjuster, small turns, squeeze to check, repeat. (likes